# npm configuration for this repository.
#
# `legacy-peer-deps` exists for exactly one dependency, and the reason is not a
# mistake in this repository.
#
# `eslint.config.mjs` lints TypeScript by pointing `typescript-eslint` at the
# TypeScript 6 API, which is installed under the alias `typescript-lint-api`.
# `typescript-eslint@8.71.1` declares a peer range of `>=4.8.4 <6.1.0`, while the
# top-level `typescript` is `7.0.2` — the native compiler, used by
# `tsc --noEmit` for its speed. npm resolves peer ranges against the declared
# tree, sees the conflict, and refuses to install.
#
# The peer is satisfied in practice. The linter genuinely runs on 6.0.3, which is
# what the alias provides and what the module-cache shim hands to
# `typescript-eslint`. Only npm's static check disagrees with reality.
#
# This is declared here rather than passed as a `--legacy-peer-deps` flag on the
# command line, because that is the alternative that quietly breaks: CI runs
# `npm ci`, a fresh clone runs `npm install`, and neither of them would carry a
# flag typed by hand. With the setting here, every install path behaves the same.
#
# The cost is honest and worth stating: while this is set, npm will not complain
# about *any* peer conflict in this project, not just this one. That is a real
# loss of a safety net, accepted because the alternative is either downgrading the
# TypeScript toolchain — a deliberate choice made here for speed — or not linting
# `src/` at all.
#
# Remove this line once `typescript-eslint` widens its peer range to include
# TypeScript 7. Upstream tracking: typescript-eslint issue #10940.
legacy-peer-deps=true
